WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.420
In 1789, Jeremy Bentham opened
a book with a bold sentence.

2
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:10.620
Nature, he wrote, has placed
mankind under two sovereign masters:

3
00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.590
pain and pleasure.

4
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.040
They point out what we ought to
do, and determine what we shall do.

5
00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.880
He even claimed a children's game
could be as valuable as poetry,

6
00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.970
if it gave as much pleasure.

7
00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:28.000
So could right and wrong be worked
out like a sum of pleasures and pains?

8
00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.780
In this mission, you'll read Bentham's own
words and test his principle of utility.

9
00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.900
Then you'll see how he
proposed to measure pleasure,

10
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.440
and where critics push back.

11
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:42.920
Your mission: find out whether morality
could be a matter of adding up.
